WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.395
You can tell a computer what you want in two ways.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.975
Imperative: you list the steps.

3
00:00:07.975 --> 00:00:12.495
Pull nginx, then start a container, then publish port 8090.

4
00:00:12.495 --> 00:00:14.793
A shell script works this way.

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.868
Declarative: you describe the result.

6
00:00:17.868 --> 00:00:22.287
There is one nginx container called tf-web, on port 8090.

7
00:00:22.287 --> 00:00:24.535
The tool works out the steps.

8
00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:26.482
Terraform is declarative.

9
00:00:26.482 --> 00:00:31.969
Your files describe the desired state: what should exist once Terraform is done.

10
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.781
Your files hold that desired state.

11
00:00:34.781 --> 00:00:39.723
Here, two resources: an nginx image, and a container called tf-web.

12
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:42.845
How does Terraform know what already exists?

13
00:00:42.845 --> 00:00:44.035
It keeps a memory.

14
00:00:44.035 --> 00:00:47.542
After every apply, it writes a file called the state.

15
00:00:47.542 --> 00:00:51.843
It records each object Terraform made, and that object's real ID.

16
00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:54.192
And then there is the real world.

17
00:00:54.192 --> 00:00:57.413
Terraform asks Docker about every ID in the state.

18
00:00:57.633 --> 00:01:00.043
Every run lines up these three pictures.

19
00:01:00.043 --> 00:01:02.873
The plan is the difference, written as actions.

20
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:03.865
First run.

21
00:01:03.865 --> 00:01:08.070
Both resources are in your files, but not in the state.

22
00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.364
So the plan says: create them.

23
00:01:10.364 --> 00:01:11.511
Plan, 2 to add.

24
00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:13.438
Apply carries out the plan.

25
00:01:13.438 --> 00:01:17.101
The image comes first, because the container refers to it.

26
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:23.505
Then apply writes the result back into the state: each address, with the real ID Docker gave it.

27
00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:26.735
Now run plan again, without touching the file.

28
00:01:26.735 --> 00:01:29.933
Terraform asks Docker about each ID in its state.

29
00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:35.423
Docker reports both objects, with the settings your file asks for.

30
00:01:35.423 --> 00:01:38.525
All three pictures agree, so the plan is empty.

31
00:01:38.733 --> 00:01:42.411
A second docker run would fail, because the name is taken.

32
00:01:42.411 --> 00:01:46.342
Terraform gives the same result however many times you run it.

33
00:01:46.342 --> 00:01:47.991
That is called idempotent.

34
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.076
Now edit the file.

35
00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:53.123
Change restart, from no, to unless-stopped.

36
00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:57.649
The container is in your files and in the state, but with other settings.

37
00:01:57.649 --> 00:01:59.896
So the plan says: change it, in place.

38
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.660
Apply makes the change.

39
00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.255
The container keeps its ID, and the state is updated.

40
00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:08.817
Last, delete the container block from your file.

41
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:12.333
It is still in the state, but gone from your files.

42
00:02:12.333 --> 00:02:14.145
So the plan says: delete it.

43
00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:18.085
Apply removes the container from Docker, and from the state.

44
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.067
Your files say what you want.

45
00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:23.642
The state remembers what Terraform made.

46
00:02:23.642 --> 00:02:25.767
Docker says what is really there.

47
00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:29.179
The plan is the difference: create, change or delete.

48
00:02:29.179 --> 00:02:31.948
And apply makes it so, then writes it down.
